## 6. Security Issue Found During Consolidation

### Issue: Data Source Pattern Leaks Secrets to State File

**Severity:** HIGH  
**Found:** February 17, 2026  
**Status:** Needs fix before continuing

#### The Problem

```hcl
# WRONG - Do not use this pattern
data "aws_secretsmanager_secret_value" "proxmox_token" {
  secret_id = "dev/proxmox/terraform-token"
}

resource "proxmox_virtual_environment_lxc" "ansible" {
  password = data.aws_secretsmanager_secret_value.proxmox_token.secret_string
}
```

**What happens:**
- Terraform fetches secret from AWS
- Stores secret value in S3 state file as plain text
- Anyone with S3 read access has your secrets
- Secrets appear in error output and logs
- Secret leaves AWS Secrets Manager and lives in state forever

#### The Fix: Inject via GitHub Actions as Environment Variables

```yaml
# In GitHub Actions workflow
steps:
  - name: Get secrets from AWS
    run: |
      PROXMOX_TOKEN=$(aws secretsmanager get-secret-value \
        --secret-id dev/proxmox/terraform-token \
        --query SecretString \
        --output text)
      
      # Mask in all logs (replaces with ***)
      echo "::add-mask::$PROXMOX_TOKEN"
      
      # Inject as Terraform variable
      echo "TF_VAR_proxmox_token=$PROXMOX_TOKEN" >> $GITHUB_ENV
```

```hcl
# In Terraform variables
variable "proxmox_token" {
  type        = string
  sensitive   = true  # Prevents printing in plan/apply output
  description = "Proxmox API token - injected from GitHub Actions"
}
```

**What this achieves:**
- Secret never written to state file
- Secret masked in all GitHub Actions logs
- Terraform marks value as sensitive in output
- State file contains only `(sensitive value)` not actual secret

#### Affected Files

Review all Terraform files for data source pattern and replace before Phase 1 deployments.

